  "verbalization": "Prednisolone is implicated in the pathogenesis of Psoriatic Arthritis through a shared mechanism involving glucocorticoids. The evidence suggests that Prednisolone, as a therapeutic glucocorticoid, is linked to Allergic Rhinitis and Atopic Dermatitis, both of which are treated by Triamcinolone. This indicates a potential pathway where Prednisolone and Triamcinolone, as members of the glucocorticoid class, exert their effects through similar molecular mechanisms, likely targeting inflammatory pathways. The lowest common ancestor of Prednisolone and Triamcinolone is the 17Alpha-Hydroxy Steroid, suggesting a common biological function in the context of inflammation and immune regulation. The connection to Psoriatic Arthritis is further supported by the indirect relationship through Allergic Rhinitis and Atopic Dermatitis, which are both treated by Triamcinolone. This integrated evidence highlights the role of glucocorticoids in modulating inflammatory responses, with Prednisolone potentially contributing to the treatment of Psoriatic Arthritis through its glucocorticoid activity.",
